The first `pack("x3 N N", ...)` assignment is overwritten on the very next line and never used. The surviving construction is correct and spec-conformant (11-byte big-endian counter || 1-byte final flag); the dead line just makes it look uncertain.
Removed the dead first pack('x3 N N', ...) assignment in Primitives::_make_nonce, together with the two "Actually, ... let's be more precise" comments that only existed to apologise for it. The surviving construction is unchanged.

Verified against the spec before deleting -- c2sp.org/age: "The first 11 bytes are a big endian chunk counter starting at zero and incrementing by one for each subsequent chunk; the last byte is 0x01 for the final chunk and 0x00 for all preceding ones." The surviving code builds exactly that: 3 zero bytes || N(counter>>32) || N(counter) = 11-byte BE counter, then C(final?1:0). The deleted line produced 11 bytes with no flag byte and was overwritten on the next line.

Tests: t/05-primitives.t now asserts the literal nonce bytes for counter 0/1/255/2**32 in both final and non-final form, and checks encrypt_payload's output for a 3-byte and a 64KiB+1 payload against ciphertext built independently with ChaCha20-Poly1305 and literal nonces -- so the chunk boundary and nonce sequence are pinned to the wire format, not to our own construction. These assertions pass identically before and after the deletion, which is the point: the cleanup is behaviour-neutral.

Verified: prove -lr t/ -> 7 files pass, 144 tests. t/04-interop.t SKIPPED (no age/rage binary here).
Committed as eb143d9 on main together with #1. Dead pack line removed; the surviving nonce construction is unchanged and now covered by literal-byte assertions in t/05-primitives.t.
